    "description": {
      "value": "<p><strong>Narrative Refactor.</strong> You can convince reality that a bad outcome was actually a different story beat.</p><ul><li>Once per scene, when your party suffers a <strong>serious setback tied to a roll</strong> (a failed group check, a botched social move, or a nasty encounter outcome), you can spend a moment in-character reframing what happened (analysis, myth, or sheer audacity).</li><li>Mechanically, the GM may <strong>downgrade</strong> the consequence by one step (catastrophic → bad, bad → mixed, mixed → mild), or allow one <strong>single reroll</strong> by a key party member.</li><li>On the campaign layer, when used in hex-scale arcs, this can justify small reductions to <strong>Darkness</strong> or hex “tilt” when the setback was more narrative than physical.</li></ul>",
      "chat": ""
    },
